<exercisegroup cols="2">
<title> Computing determinants of matrices involving variables </title>

<introduction><p>
	For each matrix:
	<ol>

		<li>
			Compute the determinant as a formula in <m>x</m> by performing row operations to simplify before computing a cofactor expansion.
		</li>

		<li> Determine the values of <m>x</m> for which the determinant value is <m>0</m>. 	</li>

	</ol>
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{bmatrix}
			x + 4 \amp  3 \amp   7   \\
			 -1   \amp  x \amp  -3   \\
			 -1   \amp -1 \amp x - 1
		\end{bmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p><ol>

		<li><p>
			One choice of a sequence of simplifying operations is:
			<ol marker="i.">
				<li><m> R_1 \leftrightarrow R_2      </m></li>
				<li><m> - R_1                        </m></li>
				<li><m> R_2 - (x + 4) R_1; R_3 + R_1 </m></li>
				<li><m> R_2 \leftrightarrow R_3       </m></li>
				<li><m> - R_2                         </m></li>
				<li><m> R_3 - (x + 3) R_2            </m>,</li>
			</ol>
			resulting in upper triangular matrix
			<md>
				\begin{bmatrix}
					1 \amp  -x   \amp      3      \\
					0 \amp x + 1 \amp   -(x + 2)  \\
					0 \amp   0   \amp {(x + 1)}^2
				\end{bmatrix}
			</md>.
			This matrix has determinant <md> {(x + 1)}^3 </md>.
			Our sequence of operations used two row swaps and two multiplications of a row by <m>-1</m>,
			all of which cancels out in terms of the effect on the determinant.
			Therefore, the original matrix has the same determinant value.
		</p></li>

		<li><p> The determinant calculated above is equal to <m>0</m> only when <m>x = -1</m>. </p></li>

	</ol></p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{bmatrix}
			x + 12 \amp  -30  \amp   60   \\
			  -5   \amp x + 7 \amp  -20   \\
			  -5   \amp   10  \amp x - 23
		\end{bmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p><ol>

		<li><p>
			One choice of a sequence of simplifying operations is:
			<ol marker="i.">
				<li><m> R_1 \leftrightarrow R_2       </m></li>
				<li><m> 5 R_2                         </m></li>
				<li><m> R_2 + (x + 12) R_1; R_3 - R_1 </m></li>
				<li><m> R_2 \leftrightarrow R_3       </m></li>
				<li><m> - R_2                         </m></li>
				<li><m> R_3 - (x + 22) R_2            </m>,</li>
			</ol>
			resulting in upper triangular matrix
			<md>
				\begin{abmatrix}{rcc}
					-5 \amp x + 7 \amp       -20       \\
					 0 \amp x - 3 \amp    -(x - 3)     \\
					 0 \amp   0   \amp (x - 3) (x + 2)
				\end{abmatrix}
			</md>.
			This matrix has determinant <md> -5 {(x - 3)}^2 (x + 2) </md>.
			From the sequence of operations,
			if the original matrix has determinant <m>d</m> then the above upper triangular matrix has determinant <m>-5 d</m>,
			from which we conclude that <md> d = {(x - 3)}^2 (x + 2) </md>.
		</p></li>

		<li><p> The determinant calculated above is equal to <m>0</m> when <m>x = 3</m> and when <m>x = -2</m>. </p></li>

	</ol></p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{bmatrix}
			x + 2 \amp  -2   \amp  -4   \\
			 -4   \amp x + 3 \amp   4   \\
			  5   \amp  -4   \amp x - 7
		\end{bmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p><ol>

		<li><p>
			One choice of a sequence of simplifying operations is the following.
			(We do not aim for upper triangular here,
			but instead aim for as many zero entries as is reasonably possible.)
			<ol marker="i.">
				<li><m> R_1 \leftrightarrow R_2        </m></li>
				<li><m> 4 R_2; 4 R_3                   </m></li>
				<li><m> R_2 + (x + 2) R_1; R_3 + 5 R_1 </m></li>
				<li><m> R_2 - R_3                      </m></li>
			</ol>
			The result of these operations is
			<md>
				\begin{abmatrix}{rcc}
					-4 \amp  x + 3  \amp     4     \\
					 0 \amp x^2 - 1 \amp     0     \\
					 0 \amp 5 x - 1 \amp 4 (x - 2)
				\end{abmatrix}
			</md>,
			which is enough zero entries to be as good as upper triangular for the purpose of computing the determinant:
			<md><mrow>
				\amp
				\det
				\begin{abmatrix}{rcc}
					-4 \amp  x + 3  \amp     4     \\
					 0 \amp x^2 - 1 \amp     0     \\
					 0 \amp 5 x - 1 \amp 4 (x - 2)
				\end{abmatrix}
			</mrow><mrow>
				\amp =
				-4 \,
				\begin{vmatrix}
					x^2 - 1 \amp     0     \\
					5 x - 1 \amp 4 (x - 2)
				\end{vmatrix}
			</mrow><mrow>
				\amp = -4 \bbrac{ (x^2 - 1) \cdot 4 (x - 2) - 0 \cdot (5 x - 1) }
			</mrow><mrow>
				\amp = -16 (x^2 - 1) (x - 2)
			</mrow></md>.
			From the sequence of operations,
			if the original matrix has determinant <m>d</m> then our simplified matrix has determinant <m>-16 d</m>,
			from which we conclude that <md> d = (x^2 - 1) (x - 2) </md>.
		</p></li>

		<li><p> The determinant calculated above is equal to <m>0</m> when <m>x = 1</m>, when <m>x = -1</m>, and when <m>x = 2</m>. </p></li>

	</ol></p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{bmatrix}
			x - 3 \amp   0   \amp  -1   \amp   0   \\
			  2   \amp x - 3 \amp  -2   \amp  -1   \\
			  0   \amp   2   \amp x - 3 \amp  -2   \\
			  2   \amp   1   \amp  -2   \amp x - 5
		\end{bmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p><ol>

		<li><p>
			One choice of a sequence of simplifying operations is the following.
			(We do not aim for upper triangular here,
			but instead aim for as many zero entries as is reasonably possible.)
			<ol marker="i.">
				<li><m> R_1 \leftrightarrow R_2                  </m></li>
				<li><m> 2 R_1                                    </m></li>
				<li><m> R_2 - (x - 3) R_1; R_4 - R_1             </m></li>
				<li><m> R_2 \leftrightarrow R_3                  </m></li>
				<li><m> 2 R_3; 2 R_4                             </m></li>
				<li><m> R_3 + {(x - 3)}^2 R_2; R_4 + (x - 4) R_2 </m></li>
			</ol>
			The result of these operations is
			<md>
				\begin{bmatrix}
					2 \amp x - 3 \amp           -2            \amp         -1         \\
					0 \amp   2   \amp          x - 3          \amp         -2         \\
					0 \amp   0   \amp {(x - 3)}^3 + 4 (x - 4) \amp -2 (x - 3) (x - 4) \\
					0 \amp   0   \amp     (x - 3) (x - 4)     \amp          0
				\end{bmatrix}
			</md>,
			which is enough zero entries to be as good as upper triangular for the purpose of computing the determinant:
			<md><mrow>
				\amp
				\det
				\begin{bmatrix}
					2 \amp x - 3 \amp           -2            \amp         -1         \\
					0 \amp   2   \amp          x - 3          \amp         -2         \\
					0 \amp   0   \amp {(x - 3)}^3 + 4 (x - 4) \amp -2 (x - 3) (x - 4) \\
					0 \amp   0   \amp     (x - 3) (x - 4)     \amp          0
				\end{bmatrix}
			</mrow><mrow>
				\amp =
				2 \,
				\begin{vmatrix}
					2 \amp          x - 3          \amp         -2         \\
					0 \amp {(x - 3)}^3 + 4 (x - 4) \amp -2 (x - 3) (x - 4) \\
					0 \amp     (x - 3) (x - 4)     \amp          0
				\end{vmatrix}
			</mrow><mrow>
				\amp =
				2 \cdot 2 \,
				\begin{vmatrix}
					{(x - 3)}^3 + 4 (x - 4) \amp -2 (x - 3) (x - 4) \\
					    (x - 3) (x - 4)     \amp          0
				\end{vmatrix}
			</mrow><mrow>
				\amp = 4 \Bbrac{ \bbrac{{(x - 3)}^3 + 4 (x - 4)} \cdot 0
			</mrow><mrow>
				\amp \phantom{=} \quad - \bbrac{-2 (x - 3) (x - 4)} \cdot \bbrac{(x - 3) (x - 4)} }
			</mrow><mrow>
				\amp = 8 {(x - 3)}^2 {(x - 4)}^2
			</mrow></md>.
			From the sequence of operations,
			if the original matrix has determinant <m>d</m> then our simplified matrix has determinant <m>8 d</m>,
			from which we conclude that <md> d = {(x - 3)}^2 {(x - 4)}^2 </md>.
		</p></li>

		<li><p> The determinant calculated above is equal to <m>0</m> when <m>x = 3</m> and when <m>x = 4</m>. </p></li>

	</ol></p></solution>

	<solution><p><ol>  <!-- determinant by transpose -->

		<li>

			<p>
			Using <xref ref="lemma-det-by-row-red-det-transpose" />,
			we can instead compute the determinant of the transpose matrix
			<md>
				\begin{bmatrix}
					x - 3 \amp   2   \amp   0   \amp   2   \\
					  0   \amp x - 3 \amp   2   \amp   1   \\
					 -1   \amp  -2   \amp x - 3 \amp  -2   \\
					  0   \amp  -1   \amp  -2   \amp x - 5
				\end{bmatrix}
			</md>.
			(Note that performing row operations on a transposed matrix is equivalent to performing <em>column</em> operations on the original matrix.)
			</p>

			<p>
			One choice of a sequence of simplifying operations for this transpose matrix is the following.
			(We do not aim for upper triangular here,
			but instead aim for as many zero entries as is reasonably possible.)
			<ol marker="i.">
				<li><m> R_1 \leftrightarrow R_3                </m></li>
				<li><m> - R_1                                  </m></li>
				<li><m> R_3 - (x - 3) R_1                      </m></li>
				<li><m> R_2 \leftrightarrow R_4                </m></li>
				<li><m> - R_2                                  </m></li>
				<li><m> R_3 + 2 (x - 4) R_2; R_4 - (x - 3) R_2 </m></li>
				<li><m> R_3 + 2 R_4                            </m></li>
			</ol>
			The result of these operations is
			<md>
				\begin{bmatrix}
					1 \amp 2 \amp   -x + 3   \amp     2     \\
					0 \amp 1 \amp      2     \amp  -x + 5   \\
					0 \amp 0 \amp  {(x - 3)}^2 \amp     0     \\
					0 \amp 0 \amp -2 (x - 4) \amp {(x - 4)}^2
				\end{bmatrix}
			</md>,
			which is enough zero entries to be as good as upper triangular for the purpose of computing the determinant:
			<md><mrow>
				\amp
				\det
				\begin{bmatrix}
					1 \amp 2 \amp   -x + 3   \amp     2     \\
					0 \amp 1 \amp      2     \amp  -x + 5   \\
					0 \amp 0 \amp  {(x - 3)}^2 \amp     0     \\
					0 \amp 0 \amp -2 (x - 4) \amp {(x - 4)}^2
				\end{bmatrix}
			</mrow><mrow>
				\amp =
				\begin{vmatrix}
					1 \amp      2     \amp  -x + 5   \\
					0 \amp  {(x - 3)}^2 \amp     0     \\
					0 \amp -2 (x - 4) \amp {(x - 4)}^2
				\end{vmatrix}
			</mrow><mrow>
				\amp =
				\begin{vmatrix}
					 {(x - 3)}^2 \amp     0     \\
					-2 (x - 4) \amp {(x - 4)}^2
				\end{vmatrix}
			</mrow><mrow>
				\amp = \Bbrac{ {(x - 3)}^2 \cdot {(x - 4)}^2 - 0 \cdot \bbrac{-2 (x - 4)} }
			</mrow><mrow>
				\amp = {(x - 3)}^2 {(x - 4)}^2
			</mrow></md>.
			Our sequence of operations used two row swaps and two multiplications of a row by <m>-1</m>,
			all of which cancels out in terms of the effect on the determinant.
			Therefore, the original matrix has the same determinant value.
			</p>

		</li>

		<li><p> The determinant calculated above is equal to <m>0</m> when <m>x = 3</m> and when <m>x = 4</m>. </p></li>

	</ol></p></solution>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
